#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124692 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f53fa522-6d50-51d9-abdd-62735575a2d1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94f53f7e-0acf-53ad-bb28-7cfb9124883a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121172} {:answer/id #uuid "b48fbcca-07de-5b03-88a9-e02fa0e16d53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121173} {:answer/id #uuid "cf01b819-643f-5aa3-b9a8-ebd9af5e2416" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121174} {:answer/id #uuid "18f84519-38c2-5381-b81a-655b79d8dca8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8.5$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121175} {:answer/id #uuid "1e7044dc-c180-54a0-8f95-b3c46d0b640d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6.5$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94f53f7e-0acf-53ad-bb28-7cfb9124883a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121172} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a0f6e5f-6571-5dc2-aafe-8dc89196cf8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121171}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "57c92fa7-df07-5594-a6b7-bd705bfe80a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-111172" :question/problem "The migration table below describes the movement of the population between cities and suburbs every year in a certain state. \n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Cities** | **Suburbs** |\n| To: | **Cities** | $0.8$ | $0.7$ |\n| **Suburbs** | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |  |\n\nThat is, $20%$ of those living in cities move to the suburbs each year, and $70%$ of those living in suburbs move to the cities.\n\nAssuming that the population of the state is $9$ million and remains constant, how many people would eventually live in cities if the given probabilities remain constant over many years?" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.8 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that $P$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. As a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the unique steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.8 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.2 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.2 & 0.7 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.7 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-0.2 & 0.7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{7}{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{2}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{9} \\\\ \\frac{2}{9}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, after many years, $\\frac{7}{9}$ of the $9$ million population will live in cities. Therefore, the total number of people living in cities after many years will be\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{9}\\cdot 9\\,\\text{million}=7\\,\\text{million}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384208 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f53fa522-6d50-51d9-abdd-62735575a2d1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c6fc6de-7dfc-5357-8fda-45910592362f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384210} {:answer/id #uuid "4040e585-d06d-5d2c-b4cc-d0968af049a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384211} {:answer/id #uuid "b1992c48-f7a6-57df-955b-18a805990c31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384212} {:answer/id #uuid "f9da4089-4c8b-5d47-962e-cf5e8c656f76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384213} {:answer/id #uuid "7acc9f03-b514-553b-8784-e2c889d11c09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7acc9f03-b514-553b-8784-e2c889d11c09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384214} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f63035f-5aea-5270-9863-f3dfdee74117" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c3f9b59b-88c0-5727-bd50-b0bfab146b30" :question/math-academy-id "q-135332" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0.6 \\\\ 1 & 0.4\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P,$ what is the value of $a-b?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0.6 \\\\ 1 & 0.4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0.6 \\\\ 1 & -0.6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0.6 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -0.6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0.6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+0.6{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{8}{5}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ \\frac{5}{8}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-b=\\frac{3}{8}-\\frac{5}{8}=-\\frac{1}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384215 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84798595-cdd4-57bf-953f-0856b041b65f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bd79e96-93b6-5775-9ce5-56ef8b44d102" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384217} {:answer/id #uuid "728f7df7-07c1-5a12-936a-231f3aeef33d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384218} {:answer/id #uuid "8eaed15e-46e6-5333-a758-12d2ceaf8ec9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384219} {:answer/id #uuid "cab41cb3-d341-5338-a715-44e774bdc44c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384220} {:answer/id #uuid "43ba3beb-6fac-5b4a-b678-27adc4ac2340" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2bd79e96-93b6-5775-9ce5-56ef8b44d102" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384217} :answer-field/id #uuid "707ee803-2e7d-5f5e-8585-e37060062d6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384216}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "079e403e-14d4-565f-89cb-9d848b0de7d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-135352" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & 0.4\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P$ shown above, what is the value of $a+b-c?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & 0.4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.6 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & -0.6 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & -0.6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.6 & 0.4 & 0.4 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & -0.6 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & -0.6 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=10{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=10{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -6 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}↔{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -6 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -10 & 14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10 & -14 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -10 & 14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{14}{10}{x}_{3}=\\frac{7}{5}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}+2(\\frac{7}{5}{x}_{3})-6{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{8}{5}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{7}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{5}{x}_{3}+\\frac{7}{5}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{20}{5}{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{7}{20} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b-c=\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{7}{20}-\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{10}{20}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384222 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f53fa522-6d50-51d9-abdd-62735575a2d1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e1736e0-1621-5f23-88fa-2d4cfec1a8cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "85\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384224} {:answer/id #uuid "fd4f12e7-2d81-55f6-a8f4-0261c521d2cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384225} {:answer/id #uuid "78faf2dc-7b80-549e-99a6-c192020b7780" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384226} {:answer/id #uuid "0d1eccd1-1a22-505b-90ab-f6e5b4cbfe87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384227} {:answer/id #uuid "77a29e3d-34ef-5f8e-a811-6414240a63ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78faf2dc-7b80-549e-99a6-c192020b7780" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384226} :answer-field/id #uuid "50858a1c-12bf-5fa3-a8ba-822f1e78454a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384223}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0ca4a36a-56ac-5467-b1dd-a66f29862b0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-135409" :question/problem "A factory produces two types of sugar: brown and extra-refined. Every year, the factory's sales executives study how customers' preferences change between the two types of sugar. The table below shows the results of this study.\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Brown** | **Extra-Refined** |\n| To: | **Brown** | $0.8$ | $1$ |\n| **Extra-Refined** | $0.2$ | $0$ |  |\n\nIt's found that $20%$ of customers who currently buy brown sugar will switch to extra-refined sugar next year, while $100%$ of customers who currently purchase extra-refined sugar will switch to brown sugar next year.\n\nAssuming that there are $450\\,000$ customers in total and that the given probabilities remain constant over many years, how many customers will eventually consume extra-refined sugar?" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.8 & 1 \\\\ 0.2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n{P}^{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0.84 & 0.8 \\\\ 0.16 & 0.2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${P}^{2}$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. As a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the unique steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.8 & 1 \\\\ 0.2 & 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.2 & 1 \\\\ 0.2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-0.2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=5{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ 6{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{6} \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{1}{6}$ of the customers will eventually use extra-refined sugar. Therefore, the number of customers who will eventually use this type of sugar is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 450\\,000=75\\,000.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384229 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84798595-cdd4-57bf-953f-0856b041b65f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee0677b4-7f71-51ee-b1c1-e260ea65735f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.9$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384231} {:answer/id #uuid "d75df1c5-4cdb-595a-b1de-4dcba024fb8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.8$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384232} {:answer/id #uuid "149ec104-b0c4-5e52-9a79-27cff048f48a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384233} {:answer/id #uuid "7b941b3a-23bb-5841-89b9-759c7bf35f90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1.2$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384234} {:answer/id #uuid "c9a8916e-4741-518b-b650-b4659b1cafd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1.6$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d75df1c5-4cdb-595a-b1de-4dcba024fb8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.8$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384232} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb12a8b1-9b6a-5b63-9685-15a4f54a9da7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384230}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b4cd14dc-370c-5165-b4c6-4dfaf5fbe2b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-135433" :question/problem "A study has recorded and analyzed the results of the parliamentary elections of a particular country, held every four years. The study shows how voter preference changes from one political party to another. The table below shows the results of this study.\n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Yellow Party** | **Blue Party** | **Red Party** |\n| To: | **Yellow Party** | $0.7$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ |\n| **Blue Party** | $0.2$ | $0.8$ | $0.3$ |  |\n| **Red Party** | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ |  |\n\nFor example, $10%$ of the people who voted for the yellow party in the last election will vote for the red party in the next election.\n\nIf the electorate contains $5.6$ million voters and the given probabilities remain constant election after election, how many people will vote for the red party after many elections have taken place?" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.7 & 0.1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.8 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.1 & 0.4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix $P$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. \nAs a result, after many elections, the distribution will eventually converge to the steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.7 & 0.1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.8 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.1 & 0.4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.3 & 0.1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.2 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.1 & -0.6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.3 & 0.1 & 0.3 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.2 & 0.3 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.1 & -0.6 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=10{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=10{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}↔{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -15 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{15}{4}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+\\frac{15}{4}{x}_{3}-6{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{9}{4}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{15}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4}{x}_{3}+\\frac{15}{4}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ 7{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{28} \\\\ \\frac{15}{28} \\\\ \\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, after many elections, $\\frac{1}{7}$ of the people will vote for the red party. Therefore, the number of people that will vote for the red party equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot 5.6\\,\\text{million}=0.8\\,\\text{million}.\n$$"}]}
